{:edb/db-before-t 2816 :edb/db-after-t 2817 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536129 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:56:23.413Z" 13194139536129 true] [17592186133801 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384991 13194139536129 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384998 13194139536129 true] [17592186133802 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385005 13194139536129 true] [17592186133813 1020 #edb/ref 17592186385012 13194139536129 true] [17592186134487 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536129 true] [17592186134487 1100 "Let $f(x, y, z) = x\\sin y + y\\cos z$. Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t) = ⟨\\sin t, t, 2t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2}]$, evaluate $∫_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$." 13194139536129 false] [17592186134487 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186134487 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))-0 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384991 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384992 13194139536129 true] [17592186384991 1089 "q-104640" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384991 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536129 true] [17592186384991 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384991 1113 #uuid "a4f9c179-1e65-5c53-aeb3-a3db01a23802" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384991 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384993 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384994 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384995 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384996 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384997 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1041 "selection" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1104 #edb/ref 17592186384995 13194139536129 true] [17592186384992 1110 #uuid "2611ed37-bc62-5fbf-bfe3-d323d798263e" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384993 1034 "2" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384993 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186384993 1111 #uuid "562b370b-afe9-5766-8214-a7ea1dba0897" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384994 1034 "\\pi +1" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186384994 1111 #uuid "4890abcc-e5a9-554f-b8b4-910775f98c77" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384995 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139536129 true] [17592186384995 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186384995 1111 #uuid "44f464f6-7582-5ef2-94eb-e0d61a14bc1f" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384996 1034 "0" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384996 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186384996 1111 #uuid "113a6345-7ba7-5755-8b3e-5859f6ad36d3" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384997 1034 "{\\pi }^{2}" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186384997 1111 #uuid "8ff8a46e-7275-5da2-83e1-cdc258f2996f" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384998 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384999 13194139536129 true] [17592186384998 1089 "q-104725" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384998 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536129 true] [17592186384998 1100 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x)\\,i+(3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384998 1113 #uuid "243f9cb0-5dde-53c8-9e73-434ecea01bc2" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384998 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,e)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}\\cdot {e}^{3}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2})-({0}^{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+{0}^{2}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{3}+1-2{e}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385000 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385001 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385002 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385003 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385004 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1041 "selection" 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1104 #edb/ref 17592186385000 13194139536129 true] [17592186384999 1110 #uuid "01376abd-2e07-55cd-abc1-84d3871cf926" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385000 1034 "{e}^{3}-2{e}^{2}+3" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385000 1111 #uuid "7664051c-6c24-518e-9117-a8f33d4cd6d5" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385001 1034 "1+{e}^{2}" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385001 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385001 1111 #uuid "4020bbe2-e5ca-545e-9842-13cc277b9e9c" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385002 1034 "{e}^{2}+{e}^{2}+1" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385002 1111 #uuid "ffe0467b-3627-5226-8292-4fc4964b427d" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385003 1034 "0" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385003 1111 #uuid "ba756780-ecb4-5d33-9b56-a0cfd2ebb420" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385004 1034 "{e}^{3}+{e}^{2}" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385004 1111 #uuid "1f870b12-6104-52e6-abdf-4afa49f8ca14" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385005 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385006 13194139536129 true] [17592186385005 1089 "q-104728" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385005 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536129 true] [17592186385005 1100 "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385005 1113 #uuid "0fc2bdb8-a693-5724-9a2a-ef8585e22781" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385005 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}-e \\\\  & =e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385007 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385008 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385009 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385010 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385011 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1041 "selection" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1104 #edb/ref 17592186385008 13194139536129 true] [17592186385006 1110 #uuid "b5327754-8f85-58eb-b5d7-7d1e9cde8615" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385007 1034 "e({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385007 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385007 1111 #uuid "43287955-309f-5e36-8662-a90b2353f679" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385008 1034 "e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1)" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385008 1111 #uuid "861080c4-68a3-5d28-9ad4-ba06f0fd7b90" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385009 1034 "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}+1}" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385009 1111 #uuid "b4c76c0f-3721-53bf-8775-c86505b77c9b" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385010 1034 "{e}^{4\\pi }" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385010 1111 #uuid "f7001c6a-06d9-58f3-ad72-8e3b7bbfd628" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385011 1034 "0" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385011 1111 #uuid "d6e4f48a-f562-570d-8c3a-0bf6cca35173" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385012 1017 #edb/ref 17592186385013 13194139536129 true] [17592186385012 1089 "q-124027" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385012 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536129 true] [17592186385012 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={e}^{x}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385012 1113 #uuid "461da5f0-629f-50fd-a47f-111b1bc8cb5d" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385012 1145 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385014 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385015 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385016 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385017 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186385018 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1041 "selection" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1104 #edb/ref 17592186385016 13194139536129 true] [17592186385013 1110 #uuid "a6a9bb57-c5d1-5935-9c4b-36805e81f3da" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385014 1034 "4e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385014 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385014 1111 #uuid "24979d9a-f5e4-53ac-8c0b-f7afaad70587" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385015 1034 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+1" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385015 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385015 1111 #uuid "ded33bb7-0bab-5955-a6b1-5e720141b4e5" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385016 1034 "{e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [17592186385016 1111 #uuid "c59fdf75-95c6-5eea-8859-8fe54df56907" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385017 1034 "{e}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" 13194139536129 true] [17592186385017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536129 true] [1759218638